A||||6||Rodney||86-88 GT quarter window choices||||1||
Z||000000||Rodney||10-24-2001||06:20 PM||||I have a Fiero owner in Germany that needs 86-88 GT quarter windows. Looking for input as to which ones to get for him. GM or the reproductions? Pros and cons of each?||||reg||1
Z||000001||Old Lar||10-24-2001||06:50 PM||||NOS windows are identical to originals.  Subject to the hairline cracks as they age as did the originals.  <A HREF="http://www.gmpartsdirect.com" TARGET=_blank>www.gmpartsdirect.com</A>  for `$130 each plus shipping<P>Reproduction are less expensive ~$200 a pair. Slighty "flatter", more resiliant, not subject to the hairline cracks or chipping like the NOS ones.  You can see the difference if you look at the NOS vs the reproduction, but for the most part, it is not noticable once the reproduction ones are installed on the car.<P>Unless you want the car to remain completely stock, I think the reproduction ones would be a better value.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Monkeyman||10-24-2001||09:30 PM||||I have a set of Doug Kinneys reproductions on my car.  I love 'em.  I actually think they look better than OEM.  Mine are tinted so that makes a difference.  Doug advertises them for $200/pair, shipped but I'm not sure if that would include shipping to Germany.  You might want to give him a quick call and ask.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Sage||10-24-2001||10:20 PM||||Agree with above posts and second MM's nomination.  Have these on my car and they work and look great.  I have the first generation windows and they are fine.  The second generation windows address the curve situation and fit and look even better.  You won't find a better person to deal with than Doug anywhere, plus the product sells itself once you see it.<P>One opinion...mine.<P><BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>------------------<BR>Have a good one!||||reg||14
Z||000004||revin||10-25-2001||08:11 AM||||I have the scoops on mine with the little window. I feel that they do cirulate the air into the engine compartment. Lots of people have said they look better than the stock plexiglass.<BR>P.S. The side scoops Doug Kinney sold me look KILLER. And I do have an incress in preformance!  Doug is the man!!!||||reg||1
Z||000005||mrfixit58||10-25-2001||08:42 AM||||Nothing looks better than the OEM stock replacements on a stock car. Remember, the OEM windows are double curved to match both the contour and shape of the car (almost bubble shaped).  That is why most all fiberglass replacements with or without scoops or portholes use original windows as the basis for pulling a mold. In my opinion, the $60 difference is well worth it. If, however, your going custom, the tinted aftermarket ones look great especially on black cars. The only down side is the "GT" doesn't show up very well.<P>------------------<BR>Roy  :D <BR>Just another stock Blue 87 GT   <BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/pennocksize.JPG">      <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/fullstaff.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000006||stsmithgt||10-25-2001||01:09 PM||||The OEM panels are made from lexan and from what I've heard, at least one type of the reproduction panels are made of plexiglass.  The lexan will crackle if they are exposed to the wrong chemicals like carb cleaner, but I suppose that would screw up a plexiglass window too.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">  Check out what type of material motorcycles use for their fairings.  That ought to give you a hint on what is durable and good looking.<P>------------------<BR>My work of art.(in progress.)    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/carpicsig.jpg"> ||||reg||6
